          %title
          .standout
            %p
              This is a sub-entry of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gromov-Witten+invariants"} Gromov-Witten invariants
            %p This entry is supposed to provide an exposition of some basic ideas underlying Gromov-Witten theory.
          %blockquote
            %p
              %strong> raw material
              \: notes taken verbatim in some seminar – needs to be polished
          %h1#contents Contents
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#intropart1"} part I: basics of moduli stacks of curves
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#intropart2"} part I: basics of Gromov-Witten theory
          %h2#intropart1 Intro Part I: basics of moduli stacks of curves
          %p
            %strong> question
            \: what is a
            = succeed "?" do
              %strong moduli problem
          %ul
            %li
              %p some kind of object;
            %li
              %p a notion of “familiy”/“deformation” of these objects
            %li
              %p
                some
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∼
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
                (possibly trivial) on the set of such families
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(B)
                over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            from this we get a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            (a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F : C^{op} \to Set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  B \mapsto S(B)/_\sim
          %p
            %strong terminology
            an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is called a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fine+moduli+space"} fine moduli space
            if the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} represented functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mi M
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Y_M : C^{op} \to Set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x \mapsto Hom(x,M)
          %p
            is isomorphic to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %p
            %strong examples
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mi complex
                    %mi line
                    %mi bundles
                    %mi over
                    %mi B
                    %mo }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi isom
                  %mo ↔
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ho
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Top
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\left\{
                  complex line bundles over B
                  \\right\}/isom
                  \\leftrightarrow
                  Hom_{Ho(Top)}(B, \mathbb{C}P^\infty)
          %p
            so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}P^\infty
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BU%28n%29"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B U(1)
            for complex line bundle. Similarly for higher rank vector bundles and Grassmannians.
          %p The analogue in the algebraic category is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mi line
                    %mi bundles
                    %mi L
                    %mi over
                    %mi B
                    %mi with
                    %mi generating
                    %mi sections
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi isom
                  %mo ↔
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi ℙ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\left\{
                  line bundles L over B with generating sections s_0,...,s_n \in \Gamma(L) \right\} / isom
                  \\leftrightarrow
                  Hom(B, \mathbb{P}^n).
          %p
            And also similarly for higher rank
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
          %p Despite these examples, in a lot of cases the functors are not representable. We’ll see some of these examples below.
          %p
            Why are
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fine+moduli+space"} fine moduli space
          %p
            They allow us to study a
            %em single
            family which tells us universal things about all families. Even if you do not care about families or deformations, moduli spaces can help, because perhaps they can tell you something about trivial families, i.e. the objects that you are studying to begin with.
          %p
            %strong example
            studying the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+ring"} cohomology ring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BO%28n%29"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B O(n)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Gr
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} =Gr_n(\mathbb{R}^\infty)
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BU%28n%29"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B U(n)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Gr
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} =Gr_n(\mathbb{C}^\infty)
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            = succeed "es," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+class"} Chern class
          %p
            Let’s look at
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
          %p the functor of families here is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi Sch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F : Sch/\mathbb{C}^{op} \to Set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ↦
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi E
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                    %mi flat
                    %mi families
                    %mi of
                    %mi elliptic
                    %mi curves
                    %mo }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  B \mapsto \left\{
                  \\array{
                  E \\ \downarrow \\ B
                  }
                  flat families of elliptic curves
                  \\right\}
          %p
            %strong> Fact
            \: there is no
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fine+moduli+space"} fine moduli space
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
          %p
            %strong why not?
          %p
            there is something called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/j-invariant"} j-invariant
            which classifies
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
          %p let
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 3
                  %mo +
                  %mi a
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E : y^2 = x^3 + a x + b
          %p
            be an [elliptic curve]] given by parameters
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/j-invariant"} j-invariant
            is the number
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mn 8
                      %msup
                        %mn 3
                        %mn 3
                      %msup
                        %mi a
                        %mn 3
                    %mrow
                      %mn 4
                      %msup
                        %mi a
                        %mn 3
                      %mo +
                      %mn 27
                      %msup
                        %mi b
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  j(E) = \frac{2^8 3^3 a^3}{4 a^3 + 27 b^2}
          %p
            so we might guess that the “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            \-line”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fine+moduli+space"} fine moduli space
            for
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
          %p
            i.e. that there is a “universal family” of elliptic curves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to \mathbb{A}^1
            such that the fiber over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j \in \mathbb{A}^1
            is the elliptic cuvre with that
            = succeed "-invariant." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
          %p
            so that for any family
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to B
            we’d have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi E
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi 𝔸
                            %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  E &amp;\to&amp; C
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  B &amp;\to&amp; \mathbb{A}^1
                  }
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \to \mathbb{A}^1
            sends a point in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/j-invariant"} j-invariant
            of the elliptic curve in the fiber over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p Now consider the family
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi χ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi ℙ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\chi = (y^2 = x(x-1)(x+\lambda)) \subset \mathbb{A}^1 \times \mathbb{A}_\lambda^1 \times \mathbb{P}^2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi χ
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msubsup
                            %mi 𝔸
                            %mi λ
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi 𝔸
                            %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\chi &amp;\to&amp; C
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\mathbb{A}^1_\lambda &amp;\to&amp; \mathbb{A}^1
                  }
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi s
                    %mn 8
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi λ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo −
                      %mi λ
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi λ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi λ
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  j(\chi_\lambda) = s^8 \frac{(\lambda^2 - \lambda + 1)^2}{\lambda^2(\lambda-1)^2}
          %p
            now send
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1_\lambda \to \mathbb{A}^1_{\lambda}
          %p
            by dually sending
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mo ↦
                  %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda \mapsto 1-\lambda
          %p
            check that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j(\chi_\lambda) = j(\chi_{1-\lambda})
          %p this induces in the fibers a map
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi χ
                  %mo →
                  %mi χ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : \chi \to \chi
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ±
                  %mi i
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\phi : (x,y,\lambda) \mapsto (1-x,\pm i y , 1-\lambda)
          %p
            this maps further to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,-y,\lambda)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1
            to be a fine moduli space, which it is not. So
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1
            at least is not a fine moduli space, even though it might look like one.
          %p so that gives some computational insight that something goes wrong
          %p
            This argument does not yet prove that there exists no moduli space of elliptic curves. It merely proves that the “j-line”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1
            can not be the moduli space of elliptic curves. However, the basic argument can be adapted, if one so desires, to in fact prove that there is no moduli space of elliptic curves.
          %p
            In fact, below we will see (in an exercise), that the
            = succeed "-line" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            %em is
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coarse+moduli+space"} coarse moduli space
            = succeed "-line" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
          %p
            %strong Abstract argument
            Yet another, more abstract way, to see that no fine moduli space can exist it to realize that since elliptic curves have nontivial automorphisms, it is possible to construct families of elliptic curves that are locally trivial families (of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ×
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \times E \to U
            for a fixed elliptic curve
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \to X
            is not globally trivial, i.e. not globally of the form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to X \to X
          %p With a bit of care this alone can be used to show that a fine moduli space cannot exist. This is often summarized by a slogan of the form
          %blockquote
            %p
              %strong Slogan
              \: nontrivial automorphisms of objects prevent the family-assignment of these objects to be representable by a fine moduli space.
          %p
            However, one has to be careful with interpreting this slogan correctly. Taken naively, the slogan alone would also seem to imply that, since
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
          %p
            %strong> How to “fix” these problems
            \.
          %ol
            %li
              %p add extra structure to the objects under consideration (e.g. add marked points) to make the automorphism groups trivial.
            %li
              %p
                instead of looking for representing
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            %li
              %p
                use
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coarse+moduli+space"} coarse moduli space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Sch
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \in Sch/\mathbb{C}
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mi M
                      %mo :
                      %mi F
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi h
                        %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi_M : F \to h_M
                such that
              %p
                a)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Spec
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi h
                        %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Spec
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Spec
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(Spec(\mathbb{C})) \to h_M(Spec \mathbb{C}) = hom(Spec \mathbb{C}, M)
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
              %p
                b) given
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M'
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mrow
                          %mi M
                          %mo ′
                      %mo :
                      %mi F
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi h
                        %mrow
                          %mi M
                          %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi_{M'} : F \to h_{M'}
                then there exists unique
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo →
                      %mi M
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \to M'
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi F
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo →
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi Ψ
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi M
                                      %mo ′
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi h
                                %mrow
                                  %mi M
                                  %mo ′
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mrow
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi Ψ
                                    %mi M
                              %mo ↘
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo ↗
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi h
                                %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array{ F &amp;&amp; \stackrel{\Psi_{M'}}{\to}&amp;&amp; h_{M'} \\ &amp; {}_{\Psi_M}\searrow &amp;&amp; \nearrow \\ &amp;&amp; h_M}
          %p
            So a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coarse+moduli+space"} coarse moduli space
            is one that at least has the right underlying set of points as the
            %em right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            has: as long as we don’t look at families but just at single things, it does give the right classification.
          %p
            %strong exercise
            show that the
            = succeed "-line" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1
            = succeed "," do
              %em is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fine+moduli+space"} fine moduli space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coarse+moduli+space"} coarse moduli space
          %p
            %strong exercise
            Show that if a coarse moduli space exists, then it is unique up to canonical isomorphism.
          %p
            %strong fact
            there exists a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coarse+moduli+space"} coarse moduli space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{g,n}
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus"} genus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            marked points and a
            %span.newWikiWord
              fine moduli stack
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/fine+moduli+stack"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{g,n}
            such that for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g,n
            we have
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{g,n}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+scheme"} smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+stack"} Deligne-Mumford stack
            \- aka
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
          %p
            %strong except
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (g,n) =
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,0), (0,1), (0,2), (1,0)
            (these are the cases where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+group"} automorphism group
            is infinite, so in these cases we don’t get a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+stack"} Deligne-Mumford stack
          %p
            %strong> “Issues”
            \:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mrow
                          %mi g
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{g,n}
                is not proper, meaning: not compact, so we don’t have, for example,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+duality"} Poincare duality
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mrow
                          %mi g
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{g,n}
                and no
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
                theory. Proper, or even better projective, schemes or stacks are just a lot easier to deal with.
            %li
              %p Sometimes one wants to study singular curves or families with degeneracies.
          %p
            Both “issues” can be “resolved” via
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+compactification"} Deligne-Mumford compactification
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\bar M_{g,n}
          %p
            which parameterizes at-most-nodal curves, that are connected, of arithmetic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus"} genus
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            smooth marked points, and the group of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphism
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar M_{g,n}
            is a smooth proper
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+stack"} Deligne-Mumford stack
          %p
            smooth here means smoothness as for
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
          %p
            Deligne and Mumford were able to prove many theorems about the ordinary moduli space of curves by studying instead the compactification. For example they were able to prove that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{g,n}
            is irreducible.
          %h1#intropart2 Intro Part II: basics of Gromov-Witten theory
          %p
            %strong Gromov
            was looking for invariants of
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            = succeed "-holomorphic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
          %p
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
            and other physicists studied
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            (e.g a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+space"} Calabi-Yau 3-fold
          %p
            we want to consider now
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus"} genus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            marked points mapping into some space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p For a fixed
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\beta \in H_2(X, \mathbb{Z}),
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  M_{g,n}(X,\beta)
          %p is the space that parameterizes maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Sigma \stackrel{f}{\to} X
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is smooth, and has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            marked points, and such that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Sigma]
            the
            %span.newWikiWord
              fundamental homology class
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/fundamental+homology+class"} ?
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi β
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f_*[\Sigma] = \beta
                  \\,.
          %p
            this is a smooth
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+stack"} Deligne-Mumford stack
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (g,n)
          %p
            similarly, write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar M_{g,n}(X,\beta)
            for the same setup but with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar M_{g,n}
            as above in part 1 (the DM compactified moduli stack). EXCEPT here we do not require that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            (with its
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            marked points) has finite automorphism group; we require instead that the MAP has finite automorphism group, which means…….(fill in)
          %p
            This is a
            %em proper
            (compact)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+stack"} Deligne-Mumford stack
            \- but
            %em not
            smooth (not even in the sense of smooth stacks!). This is very important. This is what makes the theory difficult/nontrivial/interesting.
          %p we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi pt
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\bar M_{g,n}(pt,0) = \bar M_{g,n}
          %p
            what do
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theorists
            want to do?
          %p we have evaluation maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ev
                        %mi i
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\bar M_{g,n}(X,\beta) \stackrel{ev_i}{\to} X
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            labels a marked point, and we want morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi ev
                        %mn 1
                        %mo *
                      %mo ∧
                      %msubsup
                        %mi ev
                        %mn 2
                        %mo *
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi ⋯
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %msub
                            %mover
                              %mi M
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                            %mrow
                              %mi g
                              %mo ,
                              %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi β
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mi virtual
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H^\bullet(X)^{\otimes n}
                  \\stackrel{ev^*_1 \wedge ev^*_2 \wedge \cdots}{\to}
                  H^*(\bar M_{g,n}(X,\beta))
                  \\stackrel{\int_{[\bar M_{g,n}(x,\beta)]^{virtual}}}{\to}
                  \\mathbb{C}
          %p
            There should be a virtual fundamental class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi virtual
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\bar M_{g,n}(x,\beta)]^{virtual}
            that makes the maps above into the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlation+function"} correlation function
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            (the integral would be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^*(X)
          %p
            This
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+fundamental+class"} virtual fundamental class
            in algebraic geometry was constructed by Li-Tian and Behrend-Fantechi; in symplectic geometry it was done by Li-Tian.
          %p
            Why do we want to use a “virtual” fundamental class? Because \bar M_{g,n}(x,\beta) is not smooth, the actual fundamental class may not behave very well.
            %strong
              NEEDS ELUCIDATION – it would be nice if someone could give a more complete explanation of why we need a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+fundamental+class"} virtual
              fundamental class
          %p
            the mathematical structure of GW-theory was elucidated Ruan and then by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
            and Manin in 1994.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mover
                              %mi M
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                            %mrow
                              %mi g
                              %mo ,
                              %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %mi β
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi ϕ
                      %mtr
