                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  H^\bullet(X)^{\otimes}
                  \&amp;\stackrel{I_{g,n,\beta} }{\to}&amp;
                  H^\bullet(\bar M_{g,n}(X,\beta))
                  \\stackrel{\phi_*}{\to}
                  H^*(\bar M_{g,n})
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{- \otimes \Delta \otimes -}
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  \\downarrow^\alpha
                  \\\
                  H^\bullet(X)^{\otimes n_1}
                  \\otimes H^\bullet(X) \otimes
                  H^\bullet(X) \otimes H^\bullet(X)^{\otimes n_2}
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  H^{\bullet}(\bar M_{g_1,n_1+1} \times \bar M_{g_2,n_2+1})
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^\simeq
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\simeq}
                  \\\
                  H^\bullet(X)^{\otimes n_1 + 1}
                  \\otimes
                  H^\bullet(X)^{\otimes n_2 + 1}
                  \&amp;\stackrel{\sum_{\beta_1+\beta_2 = \beta}I_{g_1,n_1+1,\beta_1} \otimes I_{g_2,n_2+1,\beta_2}}{\to}&amp;
                  H^{\bullet}(\bar M_{g_1,n_1+1}) \otimes H^\bullet (\bar M_{g_2,n_2+1}))
                  }
          %p
            where the bottom right iso uses the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kunneth+formula"} Künneth formula
          %p
            here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_a
            is a homogeneous basis of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^\bullet(X)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  g_{a b} = \int_X \Delta_a \wedge \Delta_b
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi a
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta = \sum g^{a b} \Delta_a \wedge \Delta_b
          %p where
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  g^{a b} = (g_{a b})^{-1}
          %p
            so this diagram above says that this satisfies the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sewing+law"} sewing law
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %p There are various other axioms that Gromov-Witten theory must satisfy, but the sewing law above is the most important. The hard part of all of this is constructing the virtual fundamental class, and then proving that this class indeed makes the sewing law and the other axioms (which I have omitted) satisfied.
          %p The above discussion does not yet reveal much of the rich structure of Gromov-Witten invariants. But GW invariants indeed have a very rich and beautiful mathematical structure. Indeed, if we look at just the easiest part of the theory, namely g=0, we are lead to quantum cohomology and Frobenius manifolds.
          %p There is also the mirror symmetry conjecture, which roughly posits that the GW invariants can be found via calculations that a priori seem completely unrelated.
